Keep the user sheets whole when a write fails

mergeImport() and removeImport() of the writer deleted the shared gtk.css of
the user. Then they wrote a new file of the same name in place, so the file
of the user did not exist between the two steps. Neither function read the
result of write(), so a write that stopped part way left a part file and the
function still gave 0. Both functions now take the QSaveFile pattern of
writeOwned(). They check the byte count of the write and the value of
commit(), and they give 6 when either one fails. The file of the user then
holds the old content or the new content. The write mode removes those files
when the session theme is not QtCurve. That path now gives the code, and it
takes the import line out before it deletes qtcurve.css.

The four failure functions of the emitter set one static value, and no
function cleared it. One sheet that failed therefore held the value for the
life of the process. The watch mode runs one write pass for each change of
the settings. The daemon therefore refused every pass after the first
failure, and it printed nothing. Each pass now clears the value at its first
statement. Watcher::fire() reads the exit code of the pass, and it prints one
line for a pass that fails.

Two check rows run at build time over a private home. The first row builds a
large gtk.css and runs the writer under a file size limit. The file keeps
every byte, and qtcurve.css stays. The second row starts the watch mode and
gives one pass a fault through the new variable QTC_GTK_TEST_FAULT. The pass
after it runs. Each row fails on a build that keeps the old code.

The change sits on the branch work/gtk4-theme of the local tree. The push puts
that branch on the fork wyn/qtcurve. The merge request on system/qtcurve
follows that push.

Drop this patch when upstream merges the change.

